{"from": "site:sepphoris", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-06-02-03-jesus-the-tekton", "grade": "C", "note": "Sepphoris, 6 km from Nazareth, was burnt in 4 BCE and rebuilt by Antipas as 'the ornament of all Galilee': a large building market beside the village during Jesus's presumed working years. That Jesus worked there is a scholars' suggestion, not attested."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-sepphoris", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-06-02-03-jesus-the-tekton", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 18.27): Antipas 'made it [Sepphoris] the metropolis of the country' after rebuilding - the construction that would employ a Nazareth tekton."}
{"from": "artifact:judean-desert-textiles", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-06-08-09-permitted-staff", "grade": "C", "note": "Tunics recovered from the Cave of Letters: the one tunic of the verse was standard Judean dress. The staff, sandals and wallet have no separate fetched evidence, and the instruction itself is a saying, not a practice attested by finds."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-06-20-herod-feared-john", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: 'Herod, who feared lest the great influence John had over the people might put it into his power and inclination to raise a rebellion' - the same ruler's fear of the same prisoner, though Josephus grounds it in politics, Mark in reverence for his holiness."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-240-252-antipas", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mk-06-14-herod-king", "note": "Josephus: Antipas held a tetrarchy, not a kingdom; Herodias 'pressed him' to seek the kingship from Caius (Caligula), and after Agrippa's accusation Caius 'took away from him his tetrarchy' and banished him to Lyons in Gaul. He never held the title Mark's 'King Herod' gives him."}
{"from": "artifact:denarius", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-06-37-two-hundred-denarii", "grade": "C", "note": "The denarius was the standard Roman silver coin, and the gospels use it as a day's wage for a laborer: 200 denarii is about eight months of a laborer's wages, the sum the disciples name. Regraded B to C by architect review, consistent with Matthew 20 and 22: the denarius as the standard coin and day wage is context for the sum named."}
{"from": "observation:galilee-rainy-season", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-06-39-green-grass", "grade": "C", "note": "More than 70% of Israel's rain falls between November and March and summers are rainless: grass around the Sea of Galilee is still green in the spring (Passover) season and dry by summer - the setting Mark's 'green grass' implies, not evidence for the event."}
