{"from": "claim:mk-06-37-two-hundred-denarii", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-denarius-coin"}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-40-ranks-hundreds-fifties", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-papyrus-45-mark"}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-23-half-kingdom", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-mark-6-herod"}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-02-03-jesus-the-tekton", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-13-55-tekton-son", "note": "Matthew's Nazarenes call Jesus 'the carpenter's son'; Mark's call Jesus himself 'the carpenter' (tekton). A difference between Gospels, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-05-no-mighty-work", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-13-57-prophet-without-honor", "note": "Mark explains the few deeds by the Nazarenes' unbelief and says Jesus 'could do no mighty work there'; Matthew says only that he did not do many mighty works there because of their unbelief."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-07-two-by-two", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-01-twelve-apostles", "note": "Both Gospels give the twelve authority over unclean spirits; Mark alone says they were sent two by two."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-08-09-permitted-staff", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-10-travel-gear", "note": "A famous difference: Mark permits a staff and sandals and one tunic; Matthew 10:9-10 forbids staff and sandals and forbids two tunics. A difference between Gospels, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-08-09-permitted-staff", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-09-money-belt", "note": "Mark forbids money in the purse; Matthew forbids gold, silver and copper in the money belts - the same prohibition with a different coinage list."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-13-anointed-oil", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:james-5-14", "note": "James 5:14 likewise directs the elders to anoint a sick person with oil: a biblical cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-14-herod-king", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-14-01-antipas-tetrarch", "note": "Matthew calls Herod the tetrarch at the same report; Mark calls him 'king'. A difference between Gospels, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-23-half-kingdom", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-14-07-oath-whatever-she-asks", "note": "Mark alone records the oath 'up to half of my kingdom', the style of Esther 5:3; Matthew's oath promises 'whatever you ask'."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-23-half-kingdom", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:esther-5-3", "note": "Esther 5:3: 'It shall be given you even to the half of the kingdom' - a biblical cross-reference for the oath's style, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "claim:mk-06-37-two-hundred-denarii", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-14-17-five-loaves-two-fish", "note": "Mark alone records the disciples' estimate of two hundred denarii of bread; Matthew records only the five loaves and two fish."}
